  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Strong low pressure system will bring widespread\nrainfall to the area on Thanksgiving Day.\n\nA complex upper level pattern will lead to unsettled weather \nthrough the Thanksgiving holiday across the region. Two upper \nlevel shortwave features, with one across the Gulf states, and \nanother across the central U.S. will phase, resulting the rapid \ndevelopment of a deepening low pressure system that will move up\nthe east coast through Friday.\n\nModel guidance has been trending slightly faster with the\nprogression of the low. Given this trend, rain will likely\nbegin before sunset this evening across the southern tier of \nthe forecast area, but then quickly overspread the remainder of \nthe area tonight and continue through midday Thursday. Based on\nthe 00Z REFS mean QPF, the heaviest rainfall rates will occur \nfrom midnight tonight through 10AM Thursday...then taper off by\nearly afternoon as the low moves north of the area. \n\nAll models give the forecast area a widespread, beneficial \nrainfall. Anywhere from 1 to 2 inches is becoming more likely \nwith perhaps localized higher amounts. Highest amounts favor \nlocations east of the Blue Ridge based of REFS probability\nguidance now greater than 50% of exceeding 2 inches. Any \nflooding potential still seems limited given low stream levels \nand ongoing drought conditions. However, freshly fallen leaves \ncould pose a urban flooding threat due to clogged drains and \nculverts during the heaviest rainfall period early Thursday \nmorning.\n\nAs the low continues to deepen and move north into New England\non Friday, wraparound showers and snow showers will be likely\nfor the favored western slopes. Light snowfall accumulations\npossible in western Greenbrier and extending south into NW North\nCarolina Friday night.\n\nCalmer weather returns for the weekend with temperatures warming\nback to above normal through early next week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Strong winds will accompany the passage of the \nlow pressure system.\n\nA secondary threat appears to be very gusty winds across the \nregion as a result of the rapidly deepening low. A significant \nlow level jet nearing 80kts will move overhead on Thursday, \nwhich will translate to gusty east/northeast surface winds \nbeginning late tonight and especially Thursday morning. Wind \ngusts exceeding 35 to 40mph will be common with occasional \nhigher gusts nearing 50mph along the higher ridges of the Blue \nRidge and also portions central Virginia. Given probabilities \nnow greater than 50% of wind gusts reaching Wind Advisory \ncriteria, have opted for an areawide Wind Advisory with the \npotential to upgrade a portion of the Piedmont to a targeted \nHigh Wind Warning in subsequent updates. This area of concern \nfor an upgrade would be from Charlotte County to Amherst County \nwhere a favorable barrier jet could produce gusts into the 50mph\nrange. REFS wind gusts probabilities for exceeding 50mph gusts \nin the aforementioned locations are approaching 50%...leading \nto higher confidence of this corridor receiving a period of \nhigher magnitude gusts. If these probabilities continue to\nincrease, a High Wind Warning may be needed.\n\nSignificant downsloping easterly winds in southeast West\nVirginia will also occur, so have already gone with a High Wind\nWarning for Western Greenbrier.",
